3. Early transition to turbulence
From conventional theory, we know that when the Reynolds number increases to certain values, the internal Øow undergoes a remarkable transition from laminar to turbulent regime. The origin of turbulence and the accompanying transition from laminar to turbulent Øow is of fundamental importance to the science of Øuid mechanics. In a Øow at low Reynolds number through a straight pipe of uniform crosssection and smooth walls, every Øuid particle moves with a uniform velocity along a straight path. Viscous forces slow the particles near the wall in relation to those in the center core. The Øow is well ordered and particles travel along neighboring layers (laminar Øow). However, this orderly pattern of Øow ceases to exist at higher Reynolds number and strong mixing of the particles from di erent layers takes place. The Øuctuation of streamlines at a Æxed point causes an exchange of momentum in transverse direction. As a consequence, the velocity distribution over the cross section is considerably more uniform than in laminar Øow. The most essential feature of the transition from laminar to turbulent Øow is a noticeable change in the pattern of Øow resistance. In laminar Øow, the longitudinal pressure gradient, which maintains the motion, is proportional to the Ærst power of average velocity, as can be seen from Eq. (1). By contrast, in turbulent Øow this pressure gradient becomes nearly proportional to the square of the average velocity of Øow, Schlichting (1968). This increase in the resistance to Øow results from the turbulent mixing motion. The pressure gradient ÖDP =DlÜ required to force liquid through a straight pipe of uniform cross-section generally has a relationship with the volume Øow rate, ÖDP =DlÜ / Qn, where Q is related to Re by Eq. (2). In laminar region, n à 1; in transitional Øow region 1 < n < 2 and in the turbulent Øow region n P 2, Schlichting (1968). However, the experimental results obtained in this study for Øow in microtubes in the conventional